# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 6. The axis is unchanged from cycle 5 - HELD REFERENCES, not call sites - so the
# step-2 criteria are carried over. Step 1 changed subject (from tracing how a typed path
# resolves to a record, to mapping the record's own shape and the automatic lifecycle a
# merge would have to run), so the step-1 criteria are re-cut to match it.
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight here: the scored group is fourteen places that
# are keeping a bare identifier for one of these records - in a column, a join row, a
# hand-written SQL predicate, a queued job's arguments, a cache key, a stored setting -
# which a merge that retires one record onto another would have to rewrite. Nine of the
# fourteen pin a line that never names the class. Separating a holder from a reader is
# what is being scored; a list of every place the class is mentioned is the failure.
#
# WHY step 2 credits the WIDE-LIST-THEN-CLASSIFY method rather than punishing breadth:
# the ask now tells the arm to assemble the widest list first and then decide entry by
# entry. So an answer that shows both moves - a broad candidate set, then a stated
# reason each survivor keeps a reference - is doing what was asked. What is still scored
# as a miss is handing back the broad set itself as the audit.
#
# WHY specificity demands the routine OR the declaration: several rows here are
# declarations rather than calls (a through-association, a habtm) and sit in no routine.
# So the criterion asks for the enclosing routine where there is one and the declaration
# itself where there is not - and it still refuses a bare filename.
#
# TWO steps, matching the cycle-6 two-step probe. The superseded cycle-5 rubric is at
# discourse.rubric.yaml.holder-subset-cycle5.bak, the cycle-4 session at
# discourse.rubric.yaml.session-7step-cycle4.bak, the cycle-4 probe at
# discourse.rubric.yaml.probe-2step-cycle4.bak, cycle 3 at
# discourse.rubric.yaml.declared-link-12row.bak, cycle 2 at
# discourse.rubric.yaml.mixed-22row.bak, cycle 1 at
# discourse.rubric.yaml.briefed-7step.bak.
#
# Step names MUST match scenarios/discourse.yaml exactly (the judge pairs by name).

audience: 'An AI coding agent that will use this answer to safely rework the Discourse
  category class - the container topics are filed under, carrying its own permissions,
  settings and hierarchy - so that two of these records can be merged, one retired onto
  the other. Score for that agent, not a human reader and not a docs page. A perfect
  answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things: what the record
  is made of and what the system already does automatically when one is saved, renamed
  or destroyed, each piece located; and every place in the codebase that is currently
  HOLDING a reference to one of these records in a form the merge would have to rewrite,
  each pinned to an exact file:line with the dangling or stale consequence stated. The
  holders that matter are mostly not the code named after this class, and mostly do not
  name the class on the line where they hold the reference: what they keep is a bare
  identifier sitting in a column, in a join row another model writes on their behalf,
  spliced into a hand-written query string, parked in stored configuration or in the
  arguments of a job queued yesterday, or cached as a cursor between runs. A weak answer
  re-lists every place the class is mentioned and calls that the audit, which buries the
  holders among readers that keep nothing; or collapses a run of holders into one line
  with a shared reason and no location on any of them; or gives file:line with nothing
  said about what would dangle, which leaves the agent knowing where a name appears and
  not what the merge would break.

  '
steps:
- name: Map the category contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: 'Does the answer open by naming the class exactly as it is written in the
        code, with the file:line where it is defined, and resolve the ambiguity if
        several things in the codebase share that name? That single identified subject is
        what the rest of the ticket is built on. Beyond it, is the record mapped as a
        thing a merge would have to reconcile - what it stores in its own right, which
        declared links it owns outright rather than shares, what it refuses to accept,
        and above all what the system does AUTOMATICALLY when one is saved, renamed or
        destroyed? That automatic behaviour is the part that matters most here: a merge
        has to run the retired record''s version of all of it, so an answer that lists
        stored fields and links but skips the lifecycle has missed the point of the step.
        Is the outside create/edit/delete path shown? Does the answer close on what a
        merge would have to reconcile and what the automatic behaviour means for the
        record being retired? Credit is for a map the next step can act from with small
        targeted lookups, not for the count of items.

        '
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: 'Does every place carry an exact file:line AND the routine or declaration
        it lives in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with nothing named
        around it is not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the
        character offset falls. A name given without the file:line where it is defined is
        not specific either. Is the subject''s own definition located, not merely named?

        '
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: 'Does each item carry one phrase on what it actually does - what a link
        owns, what a rule refuses, what an automatic behaviour performs on save, rename
        or destroy? Do the closing sentences talk about what a merge would have to
        reconcile and what the retired record leaves behind, rather than restating the
        names already listed?

        '
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: 'Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred
        from a name? Guessing at what an automatic behaviour does without opening it is
        worse than saying it did not check. Is completeness of the lifecycle claimed
        without saying how it was established?

        '
- name: Audit every dependent of the category contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: 'THE DISCRIMINATOR, and it is HOLDERS versus READERS. Does the audit
        actually separate the places that are KEEPING a reference to one of these records
        - a bare identifier in a column, a row in a join table some other model writes on
        their behalf, a value spliced into a hand-written query string, a number parked
        in stored configuration or in the arguments of a job queued earlier, a cursor in
        a cache - from the far larger set of places that merely look a record up, use it
        and keep nothing? The ask tells the arm to assemble the widest candidate list
        first and then classify entry by entry, so showing that broad sweep is not a
        fault - handing the broad sweep back AS the audit is. An answer that re-lists
        every place the class is mentioned, with no classification, has answered a
        different question and should be scored as having missed this one, however long
        the list is. Most holders do not name the class on the line where they hold the
        reference, so an answer built only from places the name appears will be thin
        exactly where it matters. Is the answer organised by area with each area worked
        to the end, and are areas it could not finish declared as unfinished rather than
        quietly summarised?

        '
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: 'Does EVERY holder carry its own exact file:line AND the routine it sits
        inside, or - where the holder is a declaration rather than a call - the
        declaration itself? Is the line pinned where the reference is actually HELD,
        rather than at some nearby line where the class happens to be named? Several
        holders named together on one line as a set that shares a reason is the failure
        this criterion exists to catch: however correct the shared reason is, it is one
        item, not a set of located holders, and it should be scored as one. A bare
        filename does not count.

        '
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: 'Does each holder carry one phrase on what would be left dangling, stale or
        pointed at the wrong record if the merge skipped it? The consequence must be
        about that holder''s own job - what breaks for the outbound payload already
        queued, the join rows behind an association, the counters kept per container, the
        search rows keyed by id, the cursor a batch job resumes from, the configured
        pointer a seeder reads - not a restatement of the line of code.

        '
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: 'Does the answer say how it convinced itself the audit is exhaustive, and
        name where it is likely still thin? An unqualified claim of completeness over a
        codebase this size is overconfidence. Naming an area it knows it did not finish,
        and what it left there, is worth more than a confident list that quietly stops.
        Does it say which entries it is unsure are holders rather than readers?

        '
